 "reading": "The wrist camera is the lever. With seeds, labels and physics identical to v5a, v6 scores 0.89 [0.81, 0.94] on the frozen suite against 0.10 (paired +0.79 [+0.71, +0.87], 79 vs 0 discordant, McNemar p = 3e-24) and beats every earlier best (v3 5k / v2 7.5k 0.20 -> +0.69, p = 4e-18). Every checkpoint from 2.5k (0.80) up is above any single-camera number, so the gain is not a checkpoint-selection effect. The variations move with it: camera_shift 0.86 (not separable from nominal, p = 0.63), lighting 0.85, target_relocation 0.90 (both inside the noise of nominal), camera_shift_far 0.75 (-0.14 vs nominal, p = 0.004: still the weakest view but 0.70 above v5a), blank image 0.05 (the policy uses the images). The gain-0.61 probe, which helped every single-camera recipe, now hurts (0.75, -0.14, p = 0.004): the policy no longer overshoots. Rejected steps 0.3 %, mean episode 46.6 frames (oracle 33.6). Reading against the literature: the Curse-of-Precision wrist-camera ablation (2607.23108) moved the precision floor from 2.35 to 3.85 mm; here, at a 30 mm acceptance radius that every single-camera recipe missed by centimetres, the eye-in-hand view removes the depth ambiguity of one third-person 224^2 image (2606.12334) and turns a 0.1-0.2 policy into a 0.9 policy at the same demo count and training budget (1.8x compute)."
